\section{Installation lifecycle and \code{replace}}{

The installed copy is resolved once, here, from \code{lib.loc} (default
\code{\link[=.libPaths]{.libPaths()}}), and that installation (its root, version and an identity made
from the stat() of its metadata files, never from a loaded namespace) is
recorded with the collection. Later changes to \code{\link[=.libPaths]{.libPaths()}} cannot redirect
it.

Attaching an id that is already attached or registered from the same package,
without \code{lib.loc}, \code{suffix}, \code{include} or \code{replace}, does nothing \strong{if the
recorded installation is unchanged}. The recorded installation is compared with the files on disk each time: a newer
version, a reinstall of the same version, a removal, or a library link that
now leads elsewhere all count as changed. A changed installation is an error
(class \code{projectData_stale_installation}) before anything is touched. It names
the collection, the path, the registered and current versions and the command
that switches. The same check runs before any resource is opened, so a
pending read never reads a replaced installation. A \code{lib.loc} that selects a
different installation for an existing id is likewise an error
(\code{projectData_installation_conflict}) unless \code{replace = TRUE}; any \code{lib.loc},
\code{suffix} or \code{include} for an existing id needs \code{replace = TRUE}.

\code{replace = TRUE} re-registers the collection under the \strong{same id}. The new
registration and the resulting name resolution are built and validated first;
only then are they committed, so there is never a second collection or a
second search-path entry. The old resource records and their caches are always
discarded when the installation changed, even if resource names and
descriptors are identical, so every later access, attached or explicit, reads
from the new installation. Omitted arguments keep the current collection's
settings when the same package is replaced: \code{lib.loc} defaults to the library
the collection was found in (not \code{\link[=.libPaths]{.libPaths()}}), \code{suffix} and \code{include} are
kept, and so are the primary designation and preferences. Give an argument to
change it (\code{suffix = NA} removes a suffix). Preferences and the primary must
remain valid; if they would not, the replacement fails (class
\code{projectData_replace_failed} added) and the previous registration, bindings,
preferences and attachment state are kept. Keeping those records does not
restore backing files that were already overwritten.

Replacement changes projectData's registration and bindings only. Objects you
already assigned elsewhere are not modified or repaired:
after \verb{old_cds <- cds_main; data_attach("pkg", replace = TRUE)}, \code{old_cds}
still holds the old returned object, and it may stop working if the installed
files it reads from were overwritten. See the "Lifetime of opened objects"
section of \code{\link[=data_detach]{data_detach()}}.
}

\section{Name conflicts}{

Name resolution is metadata-only. The bare name of an object that exists in
several attached collections is bound only if you designated a primary
collection (\code{\link[=data_primary]{data_primary()}}) or a preference (\code{\link[=data_prefer]{data_prefer()}}); otherwise it
is bound to an error naming the candidates. Attaching still succeeds, and the
ambiguity shows in \code{\link[=data_catalog]{data_catalog()}}. Suffix aliases always resolve and must be
unique.
}

\section{Masking}{

Objects in \code{.GlobalEnv} shadow the attached environment (it sits after
\code{.GlobalEnv}) and are never touched. \code{on_mask} controls what happens when
exposed names also exist elsewhere on the search path: \code{"warn"} (default),
\code{"error"} (checked before anything changes) or \code{"ignore"}.
}

\section{Selective attachment and sibling objects}{

\code{include} limits which objects are exposed (by object or resource name).
Opening one object loads its whole resource, so siblings from the same
resource are opened and cached together even if not exposed.
}

\section{Mapping completeness}{

The catalog's \code{origin} column says how a resource was discovered (\code{index},
\code{adapter} or \code{manifest}) and its \code{mapping} column whether the object names
were checked: \code{recorded} (named by the origin, unchecked), \code{unknown},
\code{verified} (opened or inspected, and produced) or \code{mismatch}. Unknown
mappings are never guessed. See \code{\link[=data_catalog]{data_catalog()}}.
}

\section{Script datasets (\verb{data/*.R})}{

Packages that ship non-lazy data scripts are supported. The script is run
only when its resource is first opened, by \code{\link[utils:data]{utils::data()}}, in a fresh
environment that holds only the script's outputs. Whether a dataset is a
script is decided as \code{\link[utils:data]{utils::data()}} decides it, from the lazy-load map and
file names in \verb{data/} (so when \code{x.R} and \code{x.rda} both exist, the script is
used), without opening or running anything. Only for a script is the
enclosing lookup chain built, once per opening, and never shared between
scripts; serialized, lazy-load and text datasets get a plain output
environment. The chain is the fixed set of standard startup packages, in ordinary startup order:
stats, graphics, grDevices, utils, datasets, methods, then base. Unqualified
calls such as \code{head()} or \code{model.matrix()} therefore work, and method
dispatch is unchanged. The set does not follow \code{options("defaultPackages")},
startup profiles or the current search path.

The global workspace, projectData's attached data environment and other
attached packages are not on the lookup path. Calls to any other package
must be qualified (\code{otherpkg::fn()}); otherwise opening fails with "could
not find function" and the resource is marked failed (see \code{\link[=data_retry]{data_retry()}}).
\verb{R CMD INSTALL} evaluates scripts in its own session, so an unqualified call
can install cleanly and fail only here.

Opening a script does not change the search path. \code{\link[utils:data]{utils::data()}} itself
attaches \code{utils} while it runs a script; projectData detaches that
attachment again afterwards (also on error) only when \code{utils} was not
attached beforehand. A preexisting attachment and its position are left
alone, and no other search-path entry is restored. Changes a script makes
deliberately (\code{library()}, \code{attach()}, \code{options()}, \code{setwd()}, ...) are the
script's own and are not undone, except that a script calling
\code{library(utils)} while \code{utils} is detached cannot be told apart from
\code{data()}'s attachment and is cleaned up too. The working directory is the
script's directory while it runs and is restored by \code{\link[=sys.source]{sys.source()}}.

Nothing is attached by the lookup chain. Exports are exposed lazily, as
promises for \code{package::name}, so package datasets are not retrieved merely
because their names are visible, and functions keep their own environments;
standard package namespaces are loaded if needed. Limits: the \code{Autoloads}
environment is not included; a script that calls \code{library()}, \code{attach()},
\code{\link[=globalenv]{globalenv()}} or \code{get(envir = globalenv())} can still reach or change the
session. This arrangement separates names; it is not a security sandbox.
}

